A synthesized name follows the scope that renamed it.

The context lowering answers a provider with a `__ctx_<name>` var marked
Synthesized, which html reads twice: once to decide where the binding goes,
and once to decide how a read of it is spelled. The two have to agree.

They did not for a component whose body html rendered in place, which it did
for an imported component the lowering left standing. Such a component's vars
were promoted onto `state` under a name unique to that instantiation, and a
synthesized one was promoted with the rest -- while the *read* was spelled
bare, `ir.Ident.Synthesized` meaning "no Model receiver" on this target and
being asked ahead of the rename table rather than through it. So the page
declared `state.__ctx_depth_1` and read a bare `__ctx_depth` that nothing
bound: a ReferenceError at startup, under markup that rendered right.

The lowering now inlines an imported component like a local one, and html has
no in-place path left: a component reaching its emitter is a runtime instance
or a panic. So this input holds the half of the rule that remains: an inlined
body's synthesized vars become top-level bindings renamed per instance, and a
read of that renamed name is spelled bare, which is correct.

deny * `[^.]__ctx_depth[^_]` -- every mention is the instance's `__ctx_depth__inst0`; the unrenamed name is one nothing binds

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import doc "./doc"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="depth") {
    doc.article
}
-- doc/article.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

context #depth(0)

component article ui.node {
    depth(depth + 1) {
        ui.text(value="depth {depth}")
    }
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
